    "body": "*Learner's Book: Chapter 8. Content standards: 3.1.6.*\n\n> **Before you start**\n\n*Tick the first column now. Tick the second when you can do it without help.*\n\n| **I can…** | **Before** | **After** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| change direct speech into reported speech |  |  |\n| shift tenses, pronouns and time words correctly |  |  |\n| report questions, commands and requests |  |  |\n| say when the tense does not shift |  |  |\n| form a question tag from any statement |  |  |\n| use the right pronoun and auxiliary in a tag |  |  |\n| handle the special tags: I am, let us, imperatives, negative words |  |  |\n| use rising and falling intonation on tags with meaning |  |  |\n\n> **Recall**\n\n*Answer from memory, before you open the Learner's Book.*\n\n**1.** Change to reported speech: *She said, 'I am tired.'*\n\n**2.** Add a tag: *You are coming, ______ ?*\n\n**3.** Add a tag: *She isn't ready, ______ ?*\n\n**4.** Which word replaces *tomorrow* in reported speech?\n\n**5.** Which word replaces *here* in reported speech?\n\n**6.** What is the past of *will*?\n\n**7.** Add a tag: *Let's go, ______ ?*\n\n**8.** Add a tag: *I am late, ______ ?*\n\n> **Key words**\n\n*Complete each entry.*\n\n\n\n**1.** Speech given in the speaker's exact words is … speech\n\n**2.** Speech given in the reporter's words is … speech\n\n**3.** The move of a tense one step into the past when reporting is the … shift\n\n**4.** The verb *said, asked, told* that introduces reported speech is the … verb\n\n**5.** A short question added to the end of a statement is a question\n\n**6.** A positive statement normally takes a … tag\n\n**7.** The rise or fall of the voice is\n\n**8.** Write, in your own words, why *He said the earth goes round the sun* keeps the present tense.\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n> **Guided practice — The four changes**\n\n*Follow the working. Fill in the missing steps.*\n\n- **Tense** — one step back: *am* → *was*, *went* → *had gone*, *will* → ______ .\n\n- **Pronouns** — from the speaker's view to the ______ 's view.\n\n- **Time words** — *now* → then, *today* → that day, *tomorrow* → ______ .\n\n- **Place words** — *here* → ______ , *this* → that. *She said, 'I will see you here tomorrow.'* → She said she would see me there the next day.\n\n> **Guided practice — Building a tag in three steps**\n\n*Follow the working. Fill in the missing steps.* **Auxiliary.** Take the auxiliary from the statement. If there is none, use ______ , *does* or *did*. **Polarity.** Positive statement → ______ tag. Negative statement → positive tag. **Pronoun.** Replace the subject with a ______ : *The men* → they. *The men repaired the road* → auxiliary *did*, negative, pronoun *they* → *didn't ______ ?* **Negative words count as negatives.** *Nobody came, did they?* — not *didn't they*.\n\n> **Core practice**\n\n*Everybody does this section.*\n\n**1.** Report: *'I am writing my examination next week,' said Ama.*\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n\n\n**2.** Report: *'We have finished the work,' the men said.*\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**3.** Report: *'Where do you live?' the officer asked me.*\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**4.** Report: *'Please close the gate,' she said to the boy.*\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**5.** Report: *'Don't touch the wires,' the electrician warned us.*\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**6.** State two situations in which the tense does not shift.\n\n_[answer space — 3 lines]_\n\n**7.** Add tags: (a) *She has gone home, ___?* (b) *They didn't pay, ___?* (c) *Kofi can swim, ___?*\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**8.** Add tags: (a) *Let's start, ___?* (b) *Close the door, ___?* (c) *I am next, ___?*\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**9.** Add tags: (a) *Nobody phoned, ___?* (b) *Nothing was broken, ___?* (c) *He hardly eats, ___?*\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n\n\n**10.** Say what a falling tag means and what a rising tag means.\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n> **Challenge**\n\n*Attempt these when the Core section is complete and correct.*\n\n**1.** Report a five-line conversation, using a different reporting verb each time.\n\n_[answer space — 8 lines]_\n\n**2.** Explain why *He asked me where did I live* is wrong, and give the rule.\n\n_[answer space — 4 lines]_\n\n**3.** Write six statements whose tags are irregular, with the tags, and say why each is irregular.\n\n_[answer space — 8 lines]_\n\n\n\n**4.** Take a short news report and rewrite three of its quotations as reported speech, then say what was lost.\n\n_[answer space — 6 lines]_\n\n**5.** Write a dialogue of eight lines in which tags are used three times: once for agreement, once for genuine doubt, and once to be polite.\n\n_[answer space — 8 lines]_\n\n> **Writing task**\n\nInterview an adult in your household or community about their work. Write the interview twice: first as a dialogue of about a hundred and twenty words in direct speech with correct punctuation, then as a report of about a hundred words in reported speech. Use at least four different reporting verbs, and include two question tags in the dialogue. **Plan first**\n\n| **Who I will interview and about what** | **Four questions I will ask** |\n| --- | --- |\n|  |  |\n| **Reporting verbs I will use** | **Where the two tags will go** |\n|  |  |\n\n**Now write.**\n\n\n\n_[answer space — 18 lines]_\n\n**Check your work**\n\n| **I have…** | **✓** |\n| --- | --- |\n| punctuated the direct speech correctly, with a new line for each speaker |  |\n| shifted tenses, pronouns, time and place words in the report |  |\n| used at least four different reporting verbs |  |\n| included two question tags in the dialogue |  |\n| kept the report at about a hundred words |  |\n\n> **Speaking and listening**\n\n*This task is carried out aloud. Your teacher or a partner signs it when you have done it.* In pairs, play the messenger: A whispers a sentence to B, B reports it aloud to C in reported speech. Ten sentences, scored on correct shifts.\n\n| **Date** | **Audience or partner** | **What was done well** | **What to improve** | **Signed** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  |  |  |  |\n\n\n\nHold a two-minute conversation with a partner in which you use six question tags — three falling for agreement and three rising for genuine questions. Your partner names the intonation of each.\n\n| **Date** | **Audience or partner** | **What was done well** | **What to improve** | **Signed** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  |  |  |  |\n\n> **Exam-style questions — 20 marks**\n\n*Answer in the space provided. Marks are shown in brackets.*\n\n**1.** Report: *'I am writing my examination next week,' said Ama.* **[3]**\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**2.** Report: *'Where do you live?' the officer asked me.* **[3]**\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**3.** Report: *'Don't touch the wires,' he warned us.* **[3]**\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**4.** State the three steps for forming a question tag. **[3]**\n\n_[answer space — 3 lines]_\n\n**5.** Add tags: (a) *She has gone, ___?* (b) *Nobody came, ___?* (c) *Let's begin, ___?* (d) *I am right, ___?* **[4]**\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**6.** Explain the difference in meaning between a falling and a rising question tag. **[4]**\n\n_[answer space — 3 lines]_\n\n\n\n> **Review**\n\n| **Section** | **Out of** | **My score** | **Date** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Recall | 8 |  |  |\n| Key words | 8 |  |  |\n| Core practice | 10 |  |  |\n| Challenge | 5 |  |  |\n| Writing task | 10 |  |  |\n| Exam-style | 20 |  |  |\n\n**One thing I got wrong, and why:**\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**One thing I must practise before the test:**\n\n*Teacher or parent signature:* ____________________ *Date:* ______________\n\n\n\n*Strand 3: Grammar Usage · Sub-Strand: Punctuation and Capitalisation*"
