    "body": "*Learner's Book: Chapter 13. Content standards: 4.2.2.*\n\n> **Before you start**\n\n*Tick the first column now. Tick the second when you can do it without help.*\n\n| **I can…** | **Before** | **After** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lay out a formal letter correctly |  |  |\n| lay out an informal letter correctly |  |  |\n| choose the right greeting and ending for each |  |  |\n| write a letter that states its business in the first line |  |  |\n| write a report with a heading, an account and a recommendation |  |  |\n| write an article for a newspaper or magazine |  |  |\n| fill a form accurately and completely |  |  |\n| write a curriculum vitae |  |  |\n\n> **Recall**\n\n*Answer from memory, before you open the Learner's Book.*\n\n**1.** Where does the writer's address go in a formal letter?\n\n**2.** Which ending goes with *Dear Sir*?\n\n**3.** Which ending goes with *Dear Mr Mensah*?\n\n**4.** Where does the receiver's address go in a formal letter?\n\n**5.** What does a formal letter have that an informal one does not?\n\n**6.** Name two things a report must contain\n\n**7.** What does *CV* stand for?\n\n**8.** Write today's date as it appears in a letter\n\n> **Key words**\n\n*Complete each entry.*\n\n\n\n**1.** A letter written to an official or an institution is a … letter\n\n**2.** A letter to a friend or relative is an … letter\n\n**3.** *Dear Sir* or *Dear Ama* is the\n\n**4.** *Yours faithfully* is the … or complimentary close\n\n**5.** The line naming the business of a formal letter is the\n\n**6.** A written account of something that happened, for someone who was not there, is a\n\n**7.** A short written record of a person's education and experience is a\n\n**8.** Write, in your own words, why the first sentence of a formal letter should state its business.\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n> **Guided practice — The seven parts of a formal letter**\n\n*Follow the working. Fill in the missing steps.* 1 Writer's address, top ______ . 2 Date beneath it. 3 Receiver's name and address, on the ______ . 4 Salutation: *Dear Sir* / *Dear Madam* / *Dear Mr ______* . 5 Subject heading, underlined. 6 The body — business first. 7 Subscription and signature. **The rule of endings.** Name unknown → *Yours ______* . Name used → *Yours sincerely*.\n\n> **Guided practice — Writing a report that is used**\n\n*Follow the working. Fill in the missing steps.*\n\n- **Heading** — what, where, when.\n\n- **Introduction** — why the report was ______ , and by whom.\n\n- **Account** — what happened, in ______ .\n\n- **Findings** — what the account shows.\n\n- **Recommendations** — what should now be done, and by ______ . A report with no recommendation is filed and forgotten.\n\n> **Core practice**\n\n*Everybody does this section.*\n\n**1.** Name the seven parts of a formal letter in order.\n\n_[answer space — 3 lines]_\n\n\n\n**2.** Give the correct ending for each: *Dear Sir*, *Dear Madam*, *Dear Mrs Adjei*, *Dear Kofi*.\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**3.** Write the writer's address, date and receiver's address block for a letter to your District Director of Education.\n\n_[answer space — 6 lines]_\n\n**4.** Write a subject heading and an opening sentence for a letter asking for the repair of your school's water tank.\n\n_[answer space — 3 lines]_\n\n**5.** State three differences between a formal and an informal letter.\n\n_[answer space — 3 lines]_\n\n**6.** Write the five parts of a report and say what each contains.\n\n_[answer space — 4 lines]_\n\n**7.** Write two recommendations for a report on a broken school fence, each naming who should act.\n\n_[answer space — 3 lines]_\n\n\n\n**8.** Write a headline and an opening paragraph for a school magazine article on an event you attended.\n\n_[answer space — 4 lines]_\n\n**9.** List the six headings a curriculum vitae needs.\n\n_[answer space — 3 lines]_\n\n**10.** Name three rules for filling a form.\n\n_[answer space — 3 lines]_\n\n> **Challenge**\n\n*Attempt these when the Core section is complete and correct.*\n\n**1.** Write a complete formal letter of about a hundred and eighty words to your District Assembly about a problem in your community.\n\n_[answer space — 12 lines]_\n\n\n\n_[answer space — 3 lines]_\n\n**2.** Rewrite that letter as an informal one to a relative who works at the Assembly, and list every change.\n\n_[answer space — 10 lines]_\n\n**3.** Write a report of about a hundred and fifty words on an incident at your school, ending with two recommendations naming who should act.\n\n_[answer space — 12 lines]_\n\n**4.** Write your own curriculum vitae under all six headings.\n\n_[answer space — 1 line]_\n\n\n\n_[answer space — 12 lines]_\n\n**5.** Explain why a letter of complaint that begins with three paragraphs of background often fails, and rewrite such an opening.\n\n_[answer space — 6 lines]_\n\n> **Writing task**\n\nChoose a real problem in your school or community. Write two documents about it: a formal letter of about two hundred words to the person who can solve it, with all seven parts and the business stated in the first sentence; and a report of about a hundred and fifty words for your head teacher, with a heading, an introduction, an account, findings and two recommendations naming who should act. **Plan first**\n\n| **The problem, in one sentence** | **Who can actually solve it** |\n| --- | --- |\n|  |  |\n| **What I am asking for, exactly** | **Two recommendations, each with a name** |\n\n\n\n_[answer space — 1 line]_\n\n**Now write.**\n\n_[answer space — 26 lines]_\n\n\n\n**Check your work**\n\n| **I have…** | **✓** |\n| --- | --- |\n| laid out all seven parts of the formal letter correctly |  |\n| stated my business in the first sentence |  |\n| matched the ending to the salutation |  |\n| given my report all five parts |  |\n| named who should act on each recommendation |  |\n\n> **Speaking and listening**\n\n*This task is carried out aloud. Your teacher or a partner signs it when you have done it.* Present your report aloud to a panel of three classmates acting as the school management committee. They must be able to state your two recommendations afterwards.\n\n| **Date** | **Audience or partner** | **What was done well** | **What to improve** | **Signed** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  |  |  |  |\n\nRole-play a telephone call to the office you wrote to, asking for a reply. Keep to formal register throughout; your partner notes any informality.\n\n| **Date** | **Audience or partner** | **What was done well** | **What to improve** | **Signed** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  |  |  |  |\n\n> **Exam-style questions — 20 marks**\n\n*Answer in the space provided. Marks are shown in brackets.*\n\n**1.** Name the seven parts of a formal letter in order. **[4]**\n\n_[answer space — 3 lines]_\n\n**2.** Give the correct ending for *Dear Sir* and for *Dear Mrs Adjei*, and state the rule. **[3]**\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**3.** State three differences between a formal and an informal letter. **[3]**\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**4.** Write a subject heading and a first sentence for a letter requesting the repair of a school water tank. **[3]**\n\n_[answer space — 3 lines]_\n\n**5.** Name the five parts of a report and say what each contains. **[4]**\n\n_[answer space — 4 lines]_\n\n**6.** Write two recommendations for a report on a broken school fence, each naming who should act. **[3]**\n\n_[answer space — 3 lines]_\n\n> **Review**\n\n| **Section** | **Out of** | **My score** | **Date** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Recall | 8 |  |  |\n| Key words | 8 |  |  |\n| Core practice | 10 |  |  |\n| Challenge | 5 |  |  |\n| Writing task | 10 |  |  |\n| Exam-style | 20 |  |  |\n\n**One thing I got wrong, and why:**\n\n_[answer space — 2 lines]_\n\n**One thing I must practise before the test:**\n\n_[answer space — 1 line]_\n\n\n\n*Teacher or parent signature:* ____________________ *Date:* ______________\n\n\n\n*Strand 4: Writing · Sub-Strand: Building and Presenting Knowledge*"
